w at Tossup", "Thin and dated polling (one July LV poll, D+5) leaves real uncertainty", "Bohannan's strong fundraising nearly matches the incumbent's"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a close race in an R+4 district, but a favorable Democratic environment, Bohannan's near-parity fundraising, and markets pricing her around 80% justify a Lean D call with a modest Democratic edge. The single, weeks-old poll and the incumbent's track record of narrow wins keep uncertainty high.", "watch": "The upcoming televised debates and any fresh polling could shift a race this close, especially if Miller-Meeks's breaks with her party on war powers resonate with independents.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 69 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Mariannette Miller-Meeks (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Iowa's 1st District rematch pits Republican incumbent Mariannette Miller-Meeks against Democrat Christina Bohannan, who is running for the third time. The district leans Republican by about four points, but the 2026 environment favors Democrats, and Bohannan has raised roughly $7 million with $5.4 million on hand, nearly matching the incumbent. Expert raters split between Tossup and Lean D, with several calling the seat a Democratic flip. Prediction markets price Bohannan's chances around 80 percent.\n\nThe polling record is thin: one July live-caller poll of likely voters showed Bohannan up five, and an older Democratic-sponsored poll showed a similar margin. Miller-Meeks has won her previous races narrowly and has drawn some attention for breaking with her party on Iran war powers. The race remains genuinely competitive, but the weight of evidence points to a narrow Democratic edge heading into the final stretch.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7001, "margin": 3.17}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7663, "margin": 4.0}]}